    "metaDescription": "DSCSA enforcement moves to unit-level, electronic interoperable data exchange in November 2026 — replacing PDFs and manual reconciliation with records a.",
    "h1": "DSCSA Unit Level Traceability 2026",
    "bodyHtml": "<p>DSCSA enforcement moves to unit-level, electronic interoperable data exchange in November 2026 — replacing PDFs and manual reconciliation with records a trading partner can verify without calling you.</p><h2>Why AuthiChain</h2><ul><li>EPCIS-compatible event data at the unit level, not just the case or lot</li><li>Records are Ed25519-signed Verifiable Credentials a downstream partner verifies offline, no portal login required</li><li>Every required field check runs before a record is accepted — partial or malformed data is rejected, not downgraded to a warning</li></ul><h2>How it works</h2><p>Issue a unique identifier per unit, anchor its record on-chain for tamper-evidence, and let anyone verify it with a single scan. Plans start at $49/mo.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>What changes in November 2026?</h3><p>Trading partners must exchange product tracing data electronically at the unit level using interoperable systems; manual and PDF-based processes no longer satisfy the requirement.</p><h3>Does this replace our EPCIS system?</h3><p>No — it signs and anchors the records your EPCIS system already produces so a partner can verify them independently.</p>",
    "jsonLd": {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@graph": [
        {
          "@type": "Service",
          "name": "AuthiChain — DSCSA Unit Level Traceability 2026",
