    "h1": "EUDR Deforestation Traceability Blockchain",
    "bodyHtml": "<p>The EU Deforestation Regulation requires proof that cocoa, coffee, palm oil, rubber, soy, timber, and cattle are traceable to the plot of land where they were grown — a claim that only holds up if a buyer can check it without asking you.</p><h2>Why AuthiChain</h2><ul><li>Geolocation and chain-of-custody events hashed and anchored per batch for tamper-evidence</li><li>Records are W3C Verifiable Credentials, so an importer’s compliance system can verify them without an AuthiChain account</li><li>Large and medium operators are in scope from December 30, 2026; small and micro enterprises from June 30, 2027</li></ul><h2>How it works</h2><p>Issue a unique identifier per unit, anchor its record on-chain for tamper-evidence, and let anyone verify it with a single scan. Plans start at $49/mo.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>What commodities does EUDR cover?</h3><p>Cattle, cocoa, coffee, oil palm, rubber, soy, and wood, plus derived products such as leather, chocolate, and furniture.</p><h3>Does blockchain anchoring satisfy EUDR by itself?</h3><p>No — EUDR requires a geolocation and legality due-diligence statement. Anchoring makes the supporting documentation tamper-evident; it does not replace the statement itself.</p>",
